course: dictionary
lesson: 16003
title: Validity
level: primary-6
stars: 2
explain: Validity is how true, fair or correct something is. When you check the validity of an argument, you ask whether each step follows correctly from the one before. «The validity of the argument depends on every step being right.»
explain: Validity can also mean whether something official is still accepted. «My train ticket has no validity left, because it expired yesterday.» The ticket was once valid, but now it is not. Passports and cards have a period of validity, which is the time they can be used.
explain: In science, a test has validity when it really measures what it should. «We need to check the validity of our experiment before we trust the results.» A test that measures something else by mistake is not valid, even if it looks clever.
explain: Use context to find the meaning. Ask yourself: is the sentence about being correct, or about being still accepted? Then swap in your meaning, such as «correct» or «still accepted», and check that it makes sense.
fact: Validity is a noun, and the adjective is valid.
fact: Validity means how true, fair or correct something is.
fact: In law, validity means a document, ticket or rule is official and still accepted.
fact: «The period of validity» means the time that something can still be used.
fact: In science, a fair test has validity when it measures what it is meant to measure.
exercise: «The passport's validity runs out next month.» What does validity mean here? | *Whether it is still official and accepted | The colour of the cover | How heavy the passport is | How many pages the passport has
exercise: In science, what does the validity of a test mean? | How many people take part | How long the test takes | *Whether it really measures what it should
exercise: «The validity of the argument depends on whether each step is correct.» Find and copy the word that means how correct something is. | *validity | argument | step | depends
test: «Check the validity of your ticket before you board.» What does validity mean in this sentence? | The price of the ticket | The seat number | The name of the station | *Whether the ticket is still accepted
test: Why is validity important in a fair test? | It makes the results look neater | It means more people can take part | It makes the test shorter | *It shows the test really measures what it should
exam: «Dev says the validity of his argument is strong because every step follows from the last.» What is Dev saying? | His argument has expired and is no longer used | His argument is short and easy to read | *His argument is correct and logical | His argument has been printed on a card
